+## Circle confirm-descriptor coverage
+
+Every wallet-signing site in the frontend carries a `confirm` descriptor (the object Circle's User-Controlled Wallet screen is built from). Counted from source rather than from memory:
+
+- **26 lexical `escrowWrite(...)` call sites**, of which 2 take the function name from a variable — `action.fn` in `computeMilestoneAction` and `meta.fn` in `EVIDENCE_MODES`.
+- **31 executable paths** once those two expand (24 static + 4 milestone actions + 3 evidence modes).
+
+`CreateEscrow.jsx`'s `deposit` is one of the 26; its descriptor predates the descriptor sweep and came from `feature/circle-ucw-wallets`. Any coverage claim quoting a smaller number (e.g. "24 of 24") is counting only quoted call sites, or only files changed in one PR — say which, or use the figures above.
+
 ## Open FRONTEND follow-ups (not contract, not blocking)
 
 - SE-3: frontend should listen for CrossChainLegCreditedOnArc and surface "small leg credited on Arc, withdraw here."
 - SE-6 (remaining piece): `EscrowDetail.jsx`'s `AckBanner` should block "Accept terms" when `InvoiceViewer`'s SHA-256 check fails. Verification itself now exists (see "Frontend: invoice attachment pinning (IPFS)" above) — this is only the missing gate on acknowledgement.
 
+Deferred from Round 13 Phase D — investigated, scoped, deliberately not built:
+
+- **Round 13 #17 — the protocol-fee snapshot is reachable, but not from where the descriptors read.** `escrowFeeBps` is emitted as `EscrowTermsSnapshotted` (`TrancheProtocol.sol:350`), indexed at `indexer/src/mapping.ts:208` into `Escrow.protocolFeeBps` (`indexer/schema.graphql:42`). So the "no getter, therefore gross only" premise in `mutualSettleConfirm`, `resolveDisputeConfirm`, `timeoutSettlementConfirm` and `payoutLines` is now obsolete *as a data-availability claim*. What blocks it is plumbing, not availability: those four descriptor families live on `EscrowDetail`/`ArbiterPanel`, whose `escrow` comes from the on-chain `getEscrowDetail`, and the only per-escrow Goldsky read on those pages (`fetchEscrowInvoice` → `useEscrowInvoice`) is gated on the escrow having an invoice at all three call sites, so piggybacking there would deliver the fee for invoiced escrows only. Doing it properly needs a new ungated per-escrow query/hook (`escrow(id) { protocolFeeBps }`), threading into the four families, an honest-degradation path when `VITE_GOLDSKY_ENDPOINT` is unset, and inverting four existing mutation-verified guards that currently assert no rate and no net ever appears (`ArbiterPanel.confirm.test.jsx:166-168`, `ArbiterPanel.resolve.test.jsx:82-89`, `EscrowDetail.settle.test.jsx:110-118`, plus `EscrowDetail.confirm.test.jsx:158`'s rationale comment). Pairs naturally with SE-3, which needs a per-escrow subgraph read of its own.
+- **Round 13 #18.4 — the CCTP forward-fee settings descriptor stays cautionary, deliberately.** `ProtocolSettings.jsx`'s `cctpForwardFeeConfirm` warns that the floor must stay at or above Circle's live fee without quoting one. That is not an oversight: `utils/cctpFee.js`'s `fetchForwardFee(src, dst)` is per-destination-domain and gas-priced, while the floor being set is protocol-wide, so there is no single honest number to attach. Quoting every supported domain and showing the max would be the defensible version (the floor's job is covering the worst route) at the cost of N Iris calls on a settings screen — and it would make this the only async descriptor builder in the sweep, since every other one is a pure sync function built inline at its call site.
+
 Found during the Circle confirm-descriptor sweep on `feature/confirm-descriptors`. All five are missing *behaviour*, not missing copy, which is why none was fixed in that work:
 
 - `EscrowDetail.jsx`'s `CancelCard`: the finalize button stays enabled when a milestone is `IN_REVIEW`/`DISPUTED`, so a user can submit a `mutualCancel` that is proven to revert (`CannotCancelDuringDispute`). The confirm screen now warns before signing — `cancelEscrowConfirm`'s blocked branch — but the button should be disabled in that state.
